<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>

! Founding Runbook ~ one household walk

<<~ ahu #/the-rite>>

!! 1 · What founding makes

A node founded by one operator stands as a SEED. A Nexus stands when two operators each seat their own key in a fresh charter (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). A fresh node mints its vessel identity and persona root, then seeds its corpus from the tracked bags. No prior CRDT document needs to survive. Each operator founds their own node on their own machine. A browser counts as a leaf of its human's Lararium; each device mints its own key, one face per device, and no device receives a seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fresh-state>>

!! Fresh state

A fresh founding needs only a fresh state mount and this household's tracked repository checkout at `/workspace`. For an older installation, move its homes aside by hand before founding again, as the stable founding road directs. No CLI pave or wipe command stands.

''Do'': prepare the first operator's fresh per-vessel `/state` directory and point `/workspace` at the household repository checkout, then follow this walk.

''See'': the repo carries the corpus and genesis; the new state directory holds no prior vessel home.

''Proves'': founding needs no old CRDT document, migration, clear, or rebirth.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prepare>>

!! 2 · Prepare the machine

All code runs in Docker. The host keeps the shell shim, hooks, mounts, editor, and custody copies. Use a local filesystem for both mounts; keep `/state` outside the repository and separate from `/workspace`, which points at the household repository checkout. The vessel image carries code and genesis, never household state. The app receives no Docker socket.

Set `LARES_STATE` to the first operator's vessel-home directory and `LARES_WORKSPACE` to the household's git checkout of this repository, which holds tracked `bags/` and receives `wikis/`. Export both paths in this shell before running the host preparation commands; alternatively, create the literal paths named in `lares-surface.env`. On Windows, keep both mounts inside the WSL distro. Keep a terminal attached for founding and custody credentials; the CLI refuses a credential act without a TTY.

''Do'': build the product image and create the two host directories.

```sh
export LARES_STATE="$HOME/lares-state"
export LARES_WORKSPACE="$PWD"
mkdir -p "$LARES_STATE"
docker build -f Dockerfile --target serve -t lares-surface:local .
```

Run these from the root of the household checkout; the state path shown stands as an example, and any local directory outside the checkout serves. Under PowerShell, assign `$env:LARES_STATE` and `$env:LARES_WORKSPACE` before calling the `.ps1` shim.

Use `tools/lares-container.sh` on Unix-like hosts or `tools/lares-container.ps1` on Windows. The shims read the same environment or `lares-surface.env` and route CLI work through the image.

''See'': Docker reports a successful image build; the state path exists and the workspace checkout holds tracked `bags/`.

''Proves'': the host has the image and durable homes required by the compose surface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! 3 · Decide names and custody before typing

Choose the household domain and DNS provider; name the host that the household resolver will answer. Choose a private persona label and an outward Handle for each operator. A seat needs no public Handle; choose its private chair label and where its cold-key paper rests. Name the custody holders and separate storage places before the CLI prints recovery cards. Keep domain credentials outside the vessel image, compose inputs, and state mount.

Synthetic examples below use `operator-one`, `Operator One`, and `hearth.home.example.test`. Replace each with household choices. A private label and a public Handle carry separate meanings, even when their text matches.

''Do'': write down the chosen labels, Handle, chair, domain, backup path, and custody destinations.

''See'': each decision names a holder, a location, or a persona act.

''Proves'': the founding commands can use deliberate household values rather than inventing them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rehearse>>

!! 4 · Rehearse in an isolated Docker project

The harbor witness gives each leg fresh scratch state and workspace, chooses its own Compose project, and removes only its own containers and scratch directories. It builds the local image if that tag does not exist. Give the project a unique name if another rehearsal already uses the default.

''Do'': run the harbor container walk.

```sh
HARBOR_PROJECT=founding-rehearsal tools/harbor-walk-witness.sh
```

''See'': `PASS` lines for both face-before-up and face-after-up legs, workspace projection, recipe resolution, and a final `PASS harbor walk`.

''Proves'': the shim routes a TTY-safe founding, persona, and corpus walk through the image under review. This rehearsal creates no keeper state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/found>>

!! 5 · Found the first node

The shim's founding order lights the face before `up`: found, create persona zero, wear persona zero, then bring the vessel up. `persona new` takes the credential route on the attached terminal. A persona's private label, optional public Handle, and private chair label each ride their own argument. A seat claim needs no Handle; `--chair` labels the seat privately and rides only beside `--seat`. `persona new` splits no root by default; `--split-root` arms the 2-of-3 memory split.

''Do'': on the first operator's machine, replace the synthetic values and run each line in order.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh found
tools/lares-container.sh persona new 0 --name 'operator-one' --handle 'Operator One' --seat --chair 'first-chair'
tools/lares-container.sh persona wear 0
tools/lares-container.sh up
tools/lares-container.sh vessel seed --apply --yes
tools/lares-container.sh vessel read
tools/lares-container.sh persona list --json
tools/lares-container.sh meme list --recipe default --json
```

The host shim starts the container at `up`. A face created after `up` triggers the shim's automatic restart when the CLI reports `restand`; the operator never types a restart flag. `vessel rite founding` composes found, stand, and seed, but it does not create or wear the operator's persona, so this walk uses the shim's documented order.

''See'': `vessel seed` reports its bag holdings; `vessel read` reports the live node; `persona list` marks index zero active; the daemon recipe list returns seeded wiki entries.

''Proves'': the vessel mints its identity and persona root, seeds its corpus from the tracked bags, and serves as the first operator's live node.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reserve>>

!! 6 · Establish custody

Keep a recovery route for the persona root and for the Nexus seal. The CLI prints one-time recovery material at the acts that mint it. Write each recovery card onto its intended medium and move it to the holder and place chosen in step 3. Never treat a displayed secret as a backup until the human has copied it and checked that copy.

''Do'': inspect the vault, provision and seat the Nexus quorum, inspect the resulting seal, then export a separately passphrase-sealed vault archive.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh vault status
tools/lares-container.sh nexus rite quorum
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal show
tools/lares-container.sh vault export /state/vault.sealed
mkdir -p /path/to/offline-custody
mv "$LARES_STATE/vault.sealed" /path/to/offline-custody/vault.sealed
```

The seed's seat holds its own key and commits its own cold next key, which rests on paper off the machine.

▢ ''OWED — the cold-key rite.'' The quorum rite composes `seal reserve`, `seal seat`, and `seal show`; it forges a shared reserve seed and prints three reserve cards that name generation and quorum. No verb carries a card into a rotation. `seal rotate` refuses a reserve-armed head as `reserve-armed` and refuses to arm a roll with the reserve's commit as `reserve-commit-unrevealable` (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-seal.ts`).

The credential prompts need the attached terminal. Keep the archive passphrase separate from the archive file. The export backs up the keyhive archive alone. Make a separate, consistent backup of the full state mount, including its custody slots and relation carriers.

''See'': vault status reports held slots; the quorum report names its seats and head; export reports the sealed archive path under `/state`.

''Proves'': the first operator has recorded the node's custody state and holds recovery material apart from the running machine.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/threshold-attest-recovery>>

!! Recovery cards and the guardian signing boundary

`persona new` splits no root by default. `persona new <index> --name '<label>' --split-root` opts into the 2-of-3 memory split. Reserve cards restore no charter. `lares nexus seal reserve rehearse --card-a <code> --card-b <code>` reads two reserve cards under a scratch root, proves the rebuilt key-set against the charter head's pre-commitment, writes nothing and rolls nothing. Guardians and paper restore a face's key lineage; only key material restores its private documents (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery">>).

`persona new` prints no recovery material by default; `--split-root` opts into self-custody reconstruction material and prints its own warning about shares. The persona-KEL threshold-attest core accepts independent guardian signatures; this founding supplies no guardian-enrollment command. `identity-classes.mem` tracks that unfinished path.

▢ ''OWED — reserve-backed charter rotation.'' The cold-key rite at #/reserve holds the reserve-backed rotation ruling. `stable-founding-road.mem` and `kahu.mem#/seed-and-nexus` track charter replacement and Nexus founding.

''Do'': compare each physical or offline copy with the CLI's printed destination instructions, then confirm an independent second location.

''See'': every required holder can locate the material assigned to them without access to the running node.

''Proves'': recovery material has an independent custody path. The CLI cannot prove that a human placed a card safely.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/household-reach>>

!! 7 · Establish household reach

Household HTTPS uses a DNS-01 wildcard certificate for a Kahu-held domain and one split-horizon resolver. mDNS offers a hint only. No device installs a certificate authority. Keep the DNS credential and TLS private key outside the vessel state and image.

▢ ''OWED — household HTTPS integration.'' The optional Compose profile supplies a terminator and split-horizon resolver. The operator still needs DNS-01 issuance and renewal, LAN binding and DHCP wiring, and a verified trusted origin on a household device. Carriers: `harbor.mem`, `secure-context.mem`, and `stable-founding-road.mem`.

''See'': the carriers describe the required DNS-01 wildcard, resolver, and terminator boundary; the road still tracks the integration.

''Proves'': the operator can distinguish the required design from a finished household service.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/gather-the-fleet>>

!! 8 · Add the first operator's browser, then tablet or phone leaves

The first operator starts with a browser leaf; a tablet or phone forms a separate leaf. Each device mints its own key and receives admission for that key. Admission never transfers a seed. `device-admit` accepts a public verifying key and prints a signed carry payload; it does not supply the complete browser onboarding path.

▢ ''OWED — browser and phone leaf ceremony.'' Browser crossing and render rungs run, and a leaf-side edit survives a reload. Node-side durability and the complete household phone admission walk remain OWED: secure-origin boot, on-device key minting, payload transfer, admission, and a verified live leaf. Do not use a desktop seed on a phone or call an HTTP LAN page a secure context. Measure actual device capabilities with `tools/capability-probe/`. Carriers: `stable-founding-road.mem` (browser rungs and HARBOR), `secure-context.mem`, `persona-circle.mem`, and `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/device-admit.ts`.

''See'': no supported command sequence can yet show a household phone leaf online.

''Proves'': the runbook marks the actual boundary and keeps the per-device-key law intact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/second-operator>>

!! 9 · The second operator founds a seed, then both found the Nexus

The second operator founds a separate SEED on their own machine (steps 2, 5 and 6), with their own state, persona, custody, seat and device keys. The Nexus founding ceremony runs in five steps: (1) each founder mints a seat key for the new Nexus in their own vault and its cold next key on paper; (2) the two exchange seat public keys and cold digests as files; (3) each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud, in person; (4) both compose the same fresh genesis at two of two and compare its Nexus AID; (5) each signs a carriage contract and both seats countersign it.

▢ ''OWED — the Nexus founding act.'' The CLI lacks a verb to mint and exchange independent seat keys, verify the in-person fingerprints, compose a fresh two-seat genesis, and countersign founder carriage contracts.

!!! Carriage into one household's seed (built)

This path admits the second operator as a carrier of the FIRST operator's household seed; it founds no Nexus and moves no phase. Neither operator gains a seat in the other's charter. Transfer every public file between workspaces with integrity intact.

''Do'': the second operator follows steps 2 and 5–6 on their own machine. The first operator exports the charter.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal export --no-json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/founding-roster.mem"
```

Transfer `founding-roster.mem` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator imports the first operator's charter and accepts carriage for its Nexus AID.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus seal import --carry /workspace/founding-roster.mem
tools/lares-container.sh nexus accept-carriage --nexus <nexus-aid>
```

The second operator gives the printed Nym and contract signature to the first operator. The first operator contracts that Nym and exports the resulting admit bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus contract <nym> --sig <contract-signature> --json > "$LARES_WORKSPACE/admit-bundle.json"
tools/lares-container.sh nexus members --list
```

Transfer `admit-bundle.json` to the second operator's workspace without changing its bytes. The second operator takes that bundle.

```sh
tools/lares-container.sh nexus admit-take /workspace/admit-bundle.json
```

''See'': the second operator receives a signed contract-in; the first operator's member list names the second operator after the contract act.

''Proves'': both households retain independent roots and the first household seed records the second operator's carriage membership; no Nexus founding occurs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/hosted-family-member>>

!! 10 · Host a family member's phone leaf

A further family member may ride as a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. That path still needs the same secure browser origin and per-device key property. It does not turn the family member into a second operator or transfer an operator seed.

▢ ''OWED — hosted phone-browser admission.'' The CLI supplies host and walker invite verbs. The complete phone-browser path still needs trusted HTTPS, on-device key minting, admission, and a verified live leaf. Carriers: `membership-doctrine.mem` and `stable-founding-road.mem`.

''See'': membership doctrine describes the user relation; the road tracks browser and foreign-leaf work still ahead.

''Proves'': the runbook distinguishes hosted family membership from operator-to-operator carriage.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftercare>>

!! 11 · Aftercare and the next road

Keep `/state` and `/workspace` backups distinct. The state backup holds vessel homes and custody; the workspace holds the editable corpus. Keep the sealed vault export away from both. Renew the DNS-01 certificate through the operator-owned renewal route when that path lands. Keep the host shim and its hooks on the host; keep vessel code in the image.

A live vessel may continue with its local corpus while household HTTPS, browser leaves, or user admission remain owed. The road carries these build steps and the two-household live check. The sensorium runbook carries a separate later walk.

''Do'': record backup owners and locations, then follow `stable-founding-road.mem` for the remaining HARBOR and two-household work.

''See'': the operator can name the next owner and carrier for every owed slice.

''Proves'': the first node remains legible and recoverable while the household path advances.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/key-replacement-cost>>

!! Key replacement cost

Relationships acquired after founding raise the cost of replacing keys. Preserve custody before inviting another operator.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-growth-rite>>

!! Charter succession crossing

The built `nexus seal grow` ceremony records a later charter crossing. It spans key rotation and operator signatures; this founding stops before that crossing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/identity-classes>>

!! Identity classes

A seat in a Nexus records a Kahu relation, held by a seat key apart from the persona root. The first operator and second operator each hold their own node identity and persona roots. Each browser device holds a device key minted on that device. A hosted family member holds a user leaf under an operator's Nexus. A carriage contract records an operator relation; a device admission records a device relation. No one class grants another class's seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

